# 1. STRATEGIC ARCHITECTURE

Design GoodyJS as a collection of interoperable packages under one coherent framework.

The following package layout is the initial target:

| Package | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| `goodyjs` | Main public API and recommended installation |
| `@goodyjs/core` | Runtime primitives and shared types |
| `@goodyjs/reactivity` | Signals, effects, computed values, and batching |
| `@goodyjs/ui` | Components, DOM rendering, and component composition |
| `@goodyjs/compiler` | Component compilation and template transformation |
| `@goodyjs/router` | Client-side and server-side routing |
| `@goodyjs/server` | HTTP server and request handling |
| `@goodyjs/middleware` | Middleware composition and reusable HTTP middleware |
| `@goodyjs/ssr` | Server-side rendering and hydration protocols |
| `@goodyjs/build` | Production build pipeline |
| `@goodyjs/dev` | Development server, file watching, and HMR |
| `@goodyjs/cli` | `good` command-line interface |
| `@goodyjs/testing` | Testing utilities and component test helpers |
| `@goodyjs/adapter-node` | Node.js deployment adapter |
| `@goodyjs/adapter-static` | Static export and deployment |
| `@goodyjs/adapter-worker` | Web-standard request handler for compatible runtimes |
| `@goodyjs/create` | Project scaffolding |
| `@goodyjs/compat` | Optional interoperability utilities |

This is a target architecture, not a requirement to publish 18 packages before a working prototype exists.

Start with a small number of internal modules and split packages when real dependency boundaries justify doing so.

Use a monorepo with npm workspaces or another lightweight, well-supported workspace strategy. Avoid introducing an elaborate build system merely to manage a small project.

## 1.1 Layer boundaries

Establish these boundaries:

**Core:** Types, errors, configuration, environment detection, shared utilities.

**Reactivity:** Signals, effects, dependency tracking, scheduling, cleanup.

**UI:** Components, DOM operations, events, attributes, lists, lifecycle.

**Compiler:** Parsing, validation, transformation, source maps, diagnostics.

**Router:** Route matching, parameters, navigation, loading, error handling.

**Server:** HTTP primitives, middleware, routing, body parsing, response handling.

**SSR:** HTML generation, escaping, server rendering, hydration markers, serialization.

**Build:** Module graph, dependency resolution, CSS processing, code splitting, optimization, output manifests.

**Dev:** Development server, file watching, error overlays, HMR.

**Full stack:** Shared route definitions, loaders, actions, server/client code separation, serialization, deployment.

No layer should import the entire framework when it needs only a small lower-level module.

Avoid circular dependencies. Define the dependency graph and enforce it through automated checks.

